    image: "public/HTML & CSS/Day_10.png",
    description:
      '🔥 Day 10: Labels, Placeholders & Validation in HTML\n\nForms become more user-friendly and professional when we use labels, placeholders, and validation correctly 📝\nThese features help users enter the right information and reduce errors.\n\n🔹 What is a Label?\n👉 A label describes what information the user should enter in a form field.\nExample:\n<label>Name:</label>\n<input type="text">\n\n🔥 Better Label Example\n<label for="name">\n Name:\n</label>\n<input type="text"\n id="name">\n\n🔹 Why Use Labels?\n✔ Improves accessibility\n✔ Better user experience\n✔ Helps screen readers\n✔ Clicking label focuses input field\n\n🔥 Browser Output\nName: [____________]\n\n🔹 What is a Placeholder?\n👉 A placeholder displays temporary hint text inside an input field.\nExample:\n<input type="text"\n placeholder="Enter your name">\n\n🔥 Browser Output\n[ Enter your name ]\n\n🔹 What is Validation?\n👉 Validation ensures users enter valid and required data before submitting a form.\n\nHTML5 provides built-in validation.\n🔥 Required Field Validation\n🔥 Email Validation\n🔥 Minimum & Maximum Length\n🔥 Number Validation\n🔥 Complete Example Program\n\n🔹 Real-Life Analogy\n📄 Paper Form\n✔ Label = Field Name\n✔ Placeholder = Example Input\n✔ Validation = Staff checking if form is filled correctly\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: What is the difference between a label and a placeholder?\n✅ Answer:\nA label identifies the purpose of a field.\nA placeholder provides a temporary hint/example inside the input field.\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nNever use placeholders as a replacement for labels. Labels improve accessibility and user experience 🚀\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n"Labels guide users, placeholders provide hints, and validation ensures correct data entry."',
